a bit of breaking news that we can bring  you now from uh the supreme court it's  just handed down its judgment in a case  board against google for collecting web  browsing data from iphone users well our  correspondent iva bennett is at the  supreme court and can tell us more i  mean this was quite a big case either  yeah it was it was seen as a very big  case potentially um very legally  significant because of the potential  repercussions had the court ruled  against google but in fact they have  ruled in favor of google that's happened  in the last few moments with the ruling  read out by  lord leggett they have blocked a class  action  against google and essentially maintain  the status quo on class actions  representative actions whereby they can  only be brought with the opt-in the  express say-so of every uh potential  claimant i can read you the key line  from the ruling now the supreme court  unanimously allows the appeal that  refers to the appeal  google's appeal of a ruling from the  court of appeal earlier and restores the  order made by the judge and that refers  to the original ruling by the high court  which ruled in google's favor now the  significance of this case is not the  claim itself but more the way it was  brought the substance of the claim is  that google secretly illegally uh  tracked the internet activity of  millions of iphone users between 2011  and 2012 regardless of whether or not  they had chosen the do not track option  on their safari web browser  but the supreme court was not ruling on  that instead they were ruling on whether  or not the claimant a man called richard  lloyd could bring a case on behalf of  everyone who had been affected so that's  estimated to be  4.4 million people in england and wales  if the court had ruled in his favor it  would have been illegal first allowing a  class action  without the opt-in of everyone involved  normally class actions can only be  brought with the express permission of  uh every single 